@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' Destination diagram — prose from internal/choiceui/choiceui.go and cmd/proveo/main.go's
' row-gating helpers (applicableRows, comingSoon, gateAddons, gateEvidence) lands here (batches 3 and 6).
' What the answers MEAN and how they are cached: _spec/internal/agentsettings/choice-cache.puml.
' Resolved geometry — column alignment, the divider, how a gated element is drawn:
' _spec/internal/choiceui/wireframe.puml (salt). Layout prose belongs there, not here.
' Why review needs a pty: _spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy.puml.
' Why the sandbox box is compulsory and what the escape is:
' _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml.
' The topology strip drawn under the help block: _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-strip.puml.
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── WHY HELP EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS KEYED ───────────────────────────────────
' ── A GREYED BOX THAT STATES A FACT, AND WHY IT KEEPS ITS STATE ────────────
' addonFixed are the boxes that state a FACT rather than offer a choice: their
' state is never the operator's to change, and drawing them is how the plane
' says what it EXCLUDES. "host" is the alternative an operator might otherwise
' assume unticking the daemon selects — it never is, because proveo
' containerises every run — and the TUI is the interface for the whole session
' whether or not a browser joins it.
'
' They are greyed but keep their TRUE state: a ticked greyed box reads as
' compulsory, which is what it is, and the help line says "always on" rather
' than "off" for one.
'
' Naming the ALTERNATIVE is half of what a help line is for, and the half that
' was missing. An unticked box is a posture too, and "docker (sandbox)" said
' nothing about what unticking it selects — not the host, which proveo never
' runs an agent on, but the docker backend behind egress sidecars.
'
' The egress axis is chosen by AVAILABILITY alone, not by the add-on: a harness
' that declares sbx runs there whenever the host can. Consulting the remembered
' answer offered the docker tiers — open/allowlist/review — for a run that was
' going to sbx and read the host baseline instead, hiding changeBaselineHint,
' the one true statement about what governs egress there.
'
' Help is what an option DOES, keyed by the option itself rather than indexed:
' callers assemble Options conditionally — a harness without a -browser variant
' is never offered one — and a parallel slice would then describe the wrong box.
'
' It exists because the picker could only ever explain what it had taken AWAY.
' Reason is written when something is gated off, so the two add-ons that were
' available and ticked carried no text at all, and the operator was left to
' infer "browser" and "docker (sandbox)" from their names.
'
' The block is drawn BELOW the row, never appended to it: the row is already ~70
' columns of checkboxes, so text after them runs off the terminal — which is how
' the one description the picker did have, a gated option's reason, came to end
' mid-sentence. It is WRAPPED rather than clipped, because these are sentences
' the operator is meant to read; the row's own Reason stays clipped, being a
' glance signal for a row the cursor is not on, with the full words below.
'
' ── RADIO VERSUS CHECKBOX IS A STATEMENT ABOUT REACHABLE STATES ────────────
' Brackets say "any number of these"; parentheses say "one of these". The
' execution plane is one of these, since a run has exactly one place it runs.
' Storage stays a checkbox set because "neither" is REACHABLE there — a dind
' harness with the daemon unticked reaches the agent with no daemon at all — and
' a radio cannot express it; that state draws as two empty marks, which is what
' it is. Divider is declared rather than inferred: it used to be "the first
' multi-select row", which silently meant only one group could ever be named.
'
' ── WHERE THE CURSOR MAY REST ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' A single-select row's Selected IS the choice, so it may never rest on a gated
' option. On a MULTI row it is only the cursor — the checkbox is the choice, and
' toggle refuses a gated one on its own — so the cursor may stop there. That is
' what makes a greyed box's explanation readable: it is the one whose reason the
' operator most needs, and it was the one option the cursor could never reach.
'
' ON A SCREEN TOO SMALL TO DRAW THE FORM, ONLY LEAVING IS ALLOWED. This prompt
' exists to confirm a credential and network posture, and silently CHANGING a
' posture nobody can see is worse than the truncation the viewport replaced —
' accepting one unseen is bad enough, and esc is always the answer to that.
'
' ── THE PAINT ORDER THE REGIONS DEPEND ON ──────────────────────────────────
' Topology is called at PAINT time on every frame, so it tracks the cursor —
' which `move` deliberately does not report through OnChange. It must be pure:
' it reads rows and never mutates the form it is handed, and a nil Topology
' means no strip, which is what every caller that does not want one gets free.
'
' The foot's count rides on the hint rather than taking a body line of its own:
' the rows are the scarce thing, and a region that exists to say the rows ran
' out must not be the reason one more of them did. The hint is clipped BEFORE
' the count is appended, so the count is not the first thing a narrow terminal
' cuts — on exactly the terminals where scrolling happens.
'
' The help block is drawn at y+2, not y+1: the hint sits at y and the block has
' always opened with a blank line under it. Under its own row the block shoved
' every row beneath it as the cursor moved; unreserved down here it shoved the
' figure instead, which is why the slot is reserved at its MAXIMUM height.
'
' drawTopology paints through its own pen rather than the canvas, so it has no
' window to be dropped by. The budget already guarantees the room; the guard is
' the belt to that pair of braces, because the one region outside the canvas is
' the one that could overrun it unnoticed. The pane is likewise anchored to the
' body's TOP line rather than centred: everything else here is top-anchored, and
' a centred figure would move whenever the body's height changed — re-breaking
' the thing the reserved help slot exists to hold still.
'
' headingsInWindow is the set of rows whose divider heading is on screen. Only
' those rows may drop their own label; every other row keeps it, because its
' label is the only place its name was ever going to appear. Built once per
' frame rather than rescanned per line: the body repaints at the ticker's rate.
'
' minHeight takes the WIDTH because the help slot's height depends on it. Asking
' at width zero measured the help wrapped to wrap()'s 20-column floor and
' reported a number far larger than the one layout actually tested — in the one
' region whose entire job is naming the right size.
title Choice prompt render — radio axes above the divider, checkboxes below

node "internal/choiceui" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "Form\nBanner · Title · Header · Rows" as FORM <<app>>
  component "Row\nradio: Options + Selected\ncheckbox: Multi + On/Off" as ROW <<app>>
  component "draw(screen, cursor)\nlegend · divider · markers" as DRAW <<app>>
  component "Run()\ntcell screen, one key per action" as RUN <<app>>
  component "OnChange(*Form)\nre-gated after every keystroke" as CHANGE <<app>>
  component "Selection · Selections\nread back by label" as READ <<app>>
}

node "cmd/proveo — what may be offered" as GATES <<host>> {
  component "applicableRows\ndrop an axis with nothing to decide" as APPL <<app>>
  component "comingSoon\ngrey out + move the selection off" as SOON <<app>>
  component "gateAddons · gateEvidence\ncross-row exclusivity" as GATE <<app>>
}

component "Operator terminal\nrequires a TTY" as TTY <<host>>
component "EnvHeader\ndetected secrets · settings" as HDR <<app>>
component "A locked or absent row read as an answer" as MISREAD <<error>>

GATES ARROW_MAIN FORM : rows built, then filtered
HDR ARROW_MAIN FORM
FORM ARROW_MAIN ROW
FORM ARROW_MAIN DRAW
RUN ARROW_MAIN DRAW : redraw per event
RUN ARROW_MAIN CHANGE
CHANGE ARROW_MAIN GATE
APPL ARROW_MAIN FORM
SOON ARROW_MAIN ROW
DRAW ARROW_MAIN TTY
TTY ARROW_MAIN RUN : Enter confirms · Esc/q cancels
RUN ARROW_MAIN READ
READ ARROW_ERROR MISREAD : fall back to the resolved value instead

note bottom of DRAW
  **""agent evidence"" is a toggle, not a tier.**
  Narrating more of the run costs tokens and
  screen rather than safety, so it belongs with
  the add-ons instead of under the legend, and
  ""gateEvidence"" enforces their exclusivity.
end note

note bottom of DRAW
  **Ordering is a property of the row, not of
  the prompt.** The radio axes run riskier →
  safer while a checkbox set carries no such
  direction, so a single legend cannot describe
  both and the divider ends its authority.
end note

note top of GATE
  **One gate per toggle row, run
  unconditionally.** ""gateAddons"" degrades to a
  no-op when its row was filtered out, so both
  gates fire on every keystroke and the caller
  never tracks which rows survived onto the form.
end note

note top of GATE
  **A level is one answer.** ""gateEvidence""
  clears the opposite box whenever either is
  ticked, and clearing both is allowed because
  ""evidenceFrom"" only asks whether verbose was
  selected.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  **A greyed box means one of two opposite
  things.** Unavailable is greyed AND unticked;
  compulsory is greyed AND ticked, which is the
  execution plane's sandbox — and only the
  second is read back as a selected add-on.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  **Greyed in both directions, and outside
  ""addonFixed"".** That table declares constant
  boxes, whereas an available sandbox is settled
  per run by ""sbx.Available"" — which is why the
  gate greys it, and not the declaration.
end note

note bottom of READ
  **""Selections"" drops every greyed box, so the
  compulsory one is re-added deliberately.**
  Reporting the sandbox as unselected would
  route the run to the docker backend, the exact
  posture this lock removes.
end note

note top of SOON
  **Greyed, never hidden.** ""comingSoon""
  disables the option and moves the selection
  away, yet the row still displays it with the
  reason attached, because removing it
  misrepresents an unfinished tier as absent.
end note

note bottom of SOON
  **""--egress-mode"" still accepts the value.**
  Development can therefore drive an unfinished
  tier that an operator cannot select by
  accident, and ""review"" is greyed by its
  platform with the failed constraint named.
end note

note top of TTY
  **""isStdinTTY"" gates every interactive
  prompt.** It performs a genuine ioctl check
  rather than a character-device stat, because
  ""/dev/null"" is itself a character device and
  would block a run that can never reply.
end note

note bottom of TTY
  **The add-ons picker runs BEFORE the
  missing-env wizard.** The wizard may attach a
  ""bufio\.Reader"" to stdin, and a buffered
  reader starves the picker of the keystrokes it
  is waiting for, hanging the prompt entirely.
end note

note top of HDR
  **Models are shown, not chosen.** The grid
  holds closed choices while a model id is an
  unbounded value, so the roles are printed in
  the header beside the repository instead of
  becoming a selectable row of their own.
end note

note bottom of HDR
  **Each role carries the provider it resolved
  to.** That is what exposes a credential-less
  role BEFORE launch, rather than after an
  authentication failure on its first outbound
  call.
end note

note bottom of ROW
  Checkboxes render under
  an add-ons divider.
end note

note bottom of ROW
  **The consent modal takes the whole screen.**
  ""review"" mode answers one connection at a time
  over a suspended agent TUI.
end note


@enduml
